usage: flair fusion [-h] -g GENOME -f GTF -b GENOME_ALIGNED_BAM --sample_name
                    SAMPLE_NAME [-o OUTPUT] [-t THREADS]
                    [--min_fragment_size MIN_FRAGMENT_SIZE]
                    [--min_support MIN_SUPPORT] [--max_loci MAX_LOCI]
                    [--max_dist_to_tss MAX_DIST_TO_TSS]
                    [--min_dist_between_breakpoints MIN_DIST_BETWEEN_BREAKPOINTS]
                    [--max_partners MAX_PARTNERS] [--quality QUALITY]
                    [--frac_support FRAC_SUPPORT]
                    [--no_transcriptome_alignment] [--keep_intermediate]

Identify gene fusions and generate a fusion transcriptome. Reads must be
stranded, each oriented 5' to 3' as its RNA: the 5' and 3' genes of a fusion
come from the order of a read's pieces, so a reversed read gives the fusion
backwards, which is then rejected, and poorly stranded data will miss fusions.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  -o OUTPUT, --output OUTPUT
                        output file name base for FLAIR isoforms, defaults to
                        --sample_name. The chimeric reads bams
                        OUTPUT.genomealigned.chim.bam and
                        OUTPUT.transcriptomealigned.chim.bam from an earlier
                        run with this -o are reused, whatever -b and
                        --min_fragment_size are
  -t THREADS, --threads THREADS
                        number of threads for minimap2 and for flair
                        transcriptome on the synthetic fusion genome (default:
                        4)
  --min_fragment_size MIN_FRAGMENT_SIZE
                        minimum size of alignment kept, used in minimap -s.
                        More important when doing downstream fusion detection
                        (default: 40)
  --min_support MIN_SUPPORT
                        minimum number of supporting reads for a fusion
                        (default: 3.0)
  --max_loci MAX_LOCI   max loci detected in fusion. Set higher for detection
                        of 3-gene+ fusions (default: 2)
  --max_dist_to_tss MAX_DIST_TO_TSS
                        maximum allowed distance of a fusion's 5' end to an
                        annotated transcript start of its 5' gene upstream of
                        it. With fragmented or 5' truncated reads, which start
                        far from any TSS, set this very high or to -1, which
                        does not check this (default: 15000)
  --min_dist_between_breakpoints MIN_DIST_BETWEEN_BREAKPOINTS
                        minimum allowed distance between breakpoints when they
                        are on the same strand; removes read-through
                        transcripts. A fusion from a deletion between genes
                        closer than the maximum intron length of the genome
                        alignment (minimap2 -G) aligns as one spliced read and
                        is not found: to find fusions between close genes,
                        align with a smaller minimap2 -G and lower this to
                        match (default: 100000)
  --max_partners MAX_PARTNERS
                        maximum number of fusion partners of a gene in one
                        role (5' gene, 3' gene or between them) before its
                        fusions are rejected as promiscuous. A gene with many
                        partners can be an alignment or library artifact, but
                        in highly rearranged genomes, such as cancer cell
                        lines with amplified regions, genes have many real
                        partners: in MCF7, SULF2 is the 5' gene of 5 fusions
                        and the 3' gene of ARFGEF2--SULF2. The high default
                        keeps them; lower it for genomes without substantially
                        rearranged genomes (default: 1000)
  --quality QUALITY     minimum MAPQ of every piece of a chimeric read in the
                        genome alignment for the read to count toward a
                        fusion. Applies to the genome alignments only, not to
                        the alignment of reads to annotated transcripts, where
                        a read of a gene with several overlapping isoforms has
                        a low MAPQ regardless (default: 10)
  --frac_support FRAC_SUPPORT
                        minimum fraction of a fusion's reads for a fusion
                        isoform to be reported; a fusion with one dominant
                        isoform hides its minor ones below this. Set to 0 for
                        max recall (default: 0.05)
  --no_transcriptome_alignment
                        find fusions from the chimeric reads of the genome
                        alignment only, without aligning reads to annotated
                        transcripts. About half the time and a third of the
                        memory, and -b can then hold only the chimeric reads,
                        but loses fusion reads whose partner piece the genome
                        alignment leaves clipped, particularly short 5' exons
  --keep_intermediate   keep intermediate and temporary files for debugging
                        purposes

required named arguments:
  -g GENOME, --genome GENOME
                        FastA of reference genome
  -f GTF, --gtf GTF     GTF annotation file, used for renaming FLAIR isoforms
                        to annotated isoforms and adjusting TSS/TESs
  -b GENOME_ALIGNED_BAM, --genome_aligned_bam GENOME_ALIGNED_BAM
                        bam file of reads aligned to the genome with minimap2
                        -ax splice. All reads, not only the chimeric ones:
                        reads are also aligned to annotated transcripts, which
                        finds partner pieces the genome alignment leaves
                        clipped. The chimeric reads taken from it are kept as
                        OUTPUT.genomealigned.chim.bam and
                        OUTPUT.transcriptomealigned.chim.bam, and reused if
                        they exist: with a different bam, use a new -o or
                        remove them first
  --sample_name SAMPLE_NAME
                        name of sample, will be added as metadata to output
                        files
